WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.238 --> 00:00:03.796
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.796 --> 00:00:08.501
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; There's a cancer in the education

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:09.790
system.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:14.210
Student's with this cancer are two and
a half times more likely to fail a grade.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.920
They're less able to focus,
less able to memorize, and

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.660
in a fundamental affront to
the purpose of education,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.240
this cancer inhibits a student's
ability to create new brain cells.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.550
It's not an uncommon cancer.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.050
The majority of American students
experience it to some degree by the time

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:32.780
they're teenagers.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.810
And yet, our education system doesn't set
aside the resources to deal with this.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.180
Now before I name this cancer, I want to
tell you why I'm talking to you about it.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.500
Before I came to the GSB, I lived and

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.960
worked in downtown Detroit, where I
helped start a coding boot camp company.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.380
I'm proud to say that we trained
hundreds of adults in a new career path,

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.230
software development.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.749
But what bothered me at the time was
that we were turning away hundreds more.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.820
And as I looked at their applications,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.710
I realized we weren't turning them away
because they had bad attitudes, and

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.970
it wasn't because they
lacked the right motivation.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.430
Instead, they'd been so deeply underserved
by their K through 12 education,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.400
they were incapable of
learning what we had to teach.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.450
So I looked into the best analysis and
research to understand, what is so

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.360
wrong with K through 12?

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.870
And what I found was that
the culprit isn't teacher tenure.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.120
And it isn't because there aren't
enough iPads in the classroom.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.420
Instead it's this cancer.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.919
It's called childhood trauma, and
there are many different types.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.280
&gt;From having a parent in prison, to
witnessing domestic violence in the home,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.020
to experiencing emotional neglect,
to sexual abuse.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:48.600
But the common thread,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.270
the real definition of childhood trauma
is this, it's the experience for a child,

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.340
repeated over years of their
safest relationships undermined.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.850
The people and environments that
should represent safety and security,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.460
instead become dangerous and threatening.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.470
Now, the impact of this trauma is real.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.070
People who experience it are more
likely to have heart disease,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:17.130
more likely to be depressed,
more likely to commit suicide.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.520
But one of the most pervasive and

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.380
yet least discussed impacts of this,
is its impact on learning.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.010
And to help us understand exactly
what that might look like,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.370
let's think of a case study,
a student named Shawn.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.430
Let's say Shawn's dad is in prison and

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.115
as a result his mom works long hours
to make up for that financial burden.

00:02:38.115 --> 00:02:42.405
When she comes home from work,
she's exhausted and

00:02:42.405 --> 00:02:43.750
emotionally unavailable for Shawn.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.520
Let's say Shawn wakes up some
mornings to an empty house.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:50.890
His mom is already gone to work and

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.738
he's left wondering if anyone
is looking out for him.

00:02:53.738 --> 00:02:57.850
Now as Shawn experiences these things,

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.260
his body responds in the same way that all
of our body respond to stressful events.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:06.590
His breath gets short,
his heart beats faster, and his brain,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.150
as it floods with adrenaline and cortisol
experiences a fight or flight response.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.385
It's concerned with only one thing,
getting Shawn out of the situation.

00:03:15.385 --> 00:03:18.870
It can't think any deeper than that, and

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.740
that's where Shawn's
learning gets affected.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:25.800
Because every morning when Shawn goes
to school, it looks like he's leaving

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.910
the cause of his trauma at home,
but the effects stay with him.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.840
He may look like he's
ready to learn algebra or

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.710
grapple with the big questions of history,
but his mind has become accustomed to

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.390
responding to trauma and
that doesn't stop in the classroom.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.830
His brain is physiologically
incapable of learning and

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.686
his brain chemistry becomes
the barrier to a quality education.

00:03:53.686 --> 00:03:57.512
Now, a fight response in the classroom
might look like Shawn acts out and

00:03:57.512 --> 00:03:58.399
is disruptive.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.230
A flight response might look like
he's uninterested or disengaged.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.920
And if Shawn is at a school that
hasn't trained its teachers

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.540
to recognize these things as potential
symptoms of childhood trauma,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.130
Shawn just might be ignored
as a problem child.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.783
And if he never gets to a school that
is prepared to understand what he's

00:04:18.783 --> 00:04:22.710
struggling with, he might get robbed
of an education that he deserves.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.510
His very future becomes
a victim of his trauma.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:32.540
Now, I know we're talking about
the education system here,

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.970
child protective services is supposed
to stop this trauma from happening.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.320
But what I think is so
critical to understand and

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.300
the reason why I'm standing here talking
to you today is that I think the schools

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.910
have a critical role to play in
helping students get the tools and

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:51.371
resources they need to overcome the impact
of the trauma they've already experienced.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.110
I think we need to redefine what
we consider a quality education.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.560
It's not enough to think of school
as simply transmitting information.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.650
I think schools need to prepare the minds
of the students to absorb that knowledge

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:12.280
because it doesn't matter how fantastic
your curriculum is, if you're

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:17.050
trying to teach students whose brains
are insulated by the effects of trauma.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.870
We should all care about this because
we're all stakeholders in the education

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.560
system, or taxpayers who fund it,
or current or

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.100
future employers who will hire
the people who graduate from it.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.910
And many of us are current or
future parents.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.020
We're going to send our kids into it.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:40.320
Now, if we as the stakeholders
exist in a society that

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.990
is full of people who were robbed of their
education, we shouldn't be okay with that.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.900
Now I realize that there's been
a lot of doom and gloom so far, and

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.550
I'm not a doom and gloom kind of guy.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:55.200
I have a lot of great things to share
about three schools across this country

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.170
that are doing meaningful things
to address the impact of trauma.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.040
The first one is a high
school in Washington state.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.560
They've taken an institutional approach.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.690
They've put aside the resources to
train teachers to recognize and

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.430
deal with the impact of trauma
among their students, and

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.100
hired mental health
professionals on sight.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:18.260
What this looks like is when a student
like Shawn is acting out in class,

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:22.810
his teacher doesn't ask herself
initially what's wrong with Shawn, but

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:23.610
what happened to him?

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.905
And with that is the beginning
of the conversation.

00:06:27.905 --> 00:06:31.470
Shawn can start a journey of recovery,
starting in the classroom and

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.710
culminating in the office of that
mental health professional on campus,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.780
helping him get through
what he's experienced.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.060
Another great example is
the elementary school in Texas called

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.770
the Momentus Institu.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.840
They've addressed one particular
symptom of childhood trauma,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.550
which is the inability to
regulate your emotions.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:53.870
The way they've done that is they've
turned to a trusty glitter ball.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.470
They've given every single student
a plastic ball filled with water and

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:02.340
glitter, and it holds the key to teaching
students like Shawn how to overcome

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.890
his lack of emotion like elation,
because when he gets upset or angry, he's

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:11.000
trained to first shake up that glitter
ball and not respond to his emotion.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.820
As the glitter swirls around inside the
ball, Shawn thinks about what he's feeling

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.270
and what the most productive
way to respond to that is.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.780
Once that glitter has settled, so has he.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:26.870
And over the course of years,
he learns how to overcome

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.620
the emotions he's experiencing,
and every day

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:34.740
that might have been lost to emotions that
are out of control are reclaimed by Shawn.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.760
The final example is being developed
right here at Stanford GSB.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.810
It's called Mindful Kate's Wealth,
and it's an app that allows a high

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.900
school teacher to start a day
with a mindfulness exercise.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.270
Now in addition to all of
the wonderful benefits of mindfulness,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:56.750
this activity, it's on on the phones of
students, culminates in one question.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.030
What emotion do you
feel most this morning?

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.880
The result is a roster for the teacher.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.660
Every student and every emotion.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.480
So that when Shawn has his head on his
desk in the back of the classroom,

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:13.130
his teacher knows that he reported
feeling really sad this morning and

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:18.250
instead of telling him to shape up, she
can walk beside him, ask what happened and

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:23.540
reclaim one more day of Shawn's education.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:27.390
Our education system may have a cancer,
but it's not a death sentence.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:31.670
There are meaningful things that
schools across this country are doing

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.290
to help students like Shawn overcome
the impact of their trauma.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:40.790
If we, as the stakeholders in the
education system, can help these schools

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:44.730
set aside the resources necessary to
value their students' mental health

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.569
as much as their test scores, then we
could see some dramatic improvement.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.610
If all of us as the stakeholders can
redefine how we consider quality

00:08:54.610 --> 00:09:00.640
education, then maybe we
can help give Shawn and

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.978
people like him all around this country,
their education back.

00:09:03.978 --> 00:09:06.736
Thank you.

00:09:06.736 --> 00:09:12.164
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:12.164 --> 00:09:16.559
[MUSIC]

